{
  "overall_diagnosis": "The agent updated the direct USD→CNY cell but produced an output where other numeric cells became NaN when extracted by the verifier, and the inverse CNY→USD value was not updated. This points to incomplete spreadsheet-update handling inside an embedded workbook: failing to ensure the inverse cell is correctly represented (either as a value cell that must be set, or as a formula cell that must be preserved and recalculated) and/or failing to preserve the workbook’s calculation/value state in a way that downstream extraction can read. Skill guidance likely needs clearer instructions on (a) detecting whether the inverse is formula-driven vs hardcoded and updating accordingly, (b) preserving all unaffected cells and their stored values, and (c) properly updating/repacking embedded XLSX so that verifiers reading via extraction libraries don’t see blanks/NaNs.",
  "diagnosed_skills": [
    {
      "path": "xlsx/SKILL.md",
      "confidence": 0.86,
      "diagnosis": "Current guidance emphasizes preserving formulas and avoiding errors, but does not clearly cover embedded-workbook scenarios where a verifier may read stored cell values (not recalculated formulas) and where updating one cell may require also updating a reciprocal/inverse cell if it is not formula-driven. It also lacks explicit checks to ensure all other numeric cells remain intact after save (no blanks) and guidance on maintaining cached results / calc chain behaviors so extraction returns numeric values rather than NaN.",
      "repair_instruction": "Add a section for 'Embedded Excel in Office files / verifier extraction' that instructs: (1) inspect whether the inverse rate cell is a formula or a constant; if constant, update it explicitly to 1/rate with appropriate rounding; if formula, preserve it exactly; (2) after edits, validate that all previously non-empty numeric cells remain non-empty and unchanged except intended cells; (3) ensure workbook calculation metadata is set to recalc on load and, when possible, write cached results for formulas or otherwise avoid clearing existing cached values; (4) include a recommended validation snippet using openpyxl to compare input/output cell grids (values+formulas) and to confirm no unintended blanks/NaNs."
    },
    {
      "path": "pptx/SKILL.md",
      "confidence": 0.64,
      "diagnosis": "The pptx skill explains unpacking and accessing embeddings but lacks task-specific guidance on safely editing and repacking embedded XLSX objects in PPTX while preserving all related parts (relationships, OLE packaging nuances) and verifying that the embedded object remains readable with all cell contents intact. Missing guidance may contribute to repacking that technically passes PPTX validity but yields extraction anomalies (NaNs) for unrelated cells.",
      "repair_instruction": "Add explicit workflow guidance for embedded Excel objects: locate the embedding via ppt/embeddings and corresponding .rels; edit only the embedded XLSX bytes (avoid altering other ppt structure); repack with correct relative paths and compression; and add a post-edit verification step that re-extracts the embedded XLSX from the newly packed PPTX and compares cell-by-cell with the original to ensure only intended cells changed and no cells became blank."
    }
  ],
  "unselected_skills": []
}
